# Match-three scene.
#
# Parsed when the scene loads. Every non-blank line is "key value"; anything
# after '#' is a comment. Unknown keys fail at load time.
#
# The scene is the single source of truth for the world: the engine reads it,
# the behaviour compiler reads it, and the compiled slot count is checked
# against it at load.

scene.name match3

# Sprite sheets on disk, written by tools/build_gems.py. The count is declared
# here so the behaviour compiler can hand every sprite a number without reading
# the art; the loader checks each file against it.
sheet blue BLUE.SPR 120
sheet cyan CYAN.SPR 120
sheet green GREEN.SPR 120
sheet purple PURPLE.SPR 120
sheet red RED.SPR 120
sheet yellow YELLOW.SPR 120
sheet ui UI.SPR 2

# Named sprites. The behaviour program refers to these as $name, and the handle
# is the sprite's position here, in declaration order. The third number is the
# first cel and the fourth how many the sprite animates through; one means a
# still picture.
#
# Each sheet holds 60 cels of glimmer followed by 60 of idle. The order here
# matters: the behaviour relies on glimmer N being handle N and idle N being
# handle N + 6, so it can tell them apart with a single subtraction and recover
# a gem's kind with a modulus.
#
# 'resident' decodes the whole run up front and keeps it. That is what makes a
# hundred gems at a hundred phases affordable: every gem is at a different cel,
# so without it each frame would decode a hundred of them, at about a third of a
# millisecond each. The glimmer runs are not resident because only one gem
# glimmers at a time, so its cels come and go through the ordinary cache.
sprite glimmer0 blue 0 60
sprite glimmer1 cyan 0 60
sprite glimmer2 green 0 60
sprite glimmer3 purple 0 60
sprite glimmer4 red 0 60
sprite glimmer5 yellow 0 60
sprite idle0 blue 60 60 resident
sprite idle1 cyan 60 60 resident
sprite idle2 green 60 60 resident
sprite idle3 purple 60 60 resident
sprite idle4 red 60 60 resident
sprite idle5 yellow 60 60 resident
sprite cursor ui 0
sprite picked ui 1

# The art is rendered at 60 frames per second and every second frame is kept, so
# 30 frames a second; holding each for four 60 Hz ticks plays it at half speed.
animation.ticks 4

entities.count 104

color.background 253

world.seed 4231

# Named globals. These follow the entity pool, in this order, and are what the
# behaviour program names with '@name' and '!name'.
slot cursor-row 5
slot cursor-column 5
slot picked-row -1
slot picked-column -1

# Scratch shared by the behaviour's helper definitions.
slot temp-a 0
slot temp-b 0
slot temp-c 0
slot temp-d 0

# The state machine: 0 idle, 1 swapping, 2 clearing, 3 falling.
slot phase 0
slot timer 0
slot step-x 0
slot step-y 0
slot moving 0
slot matches 0

# Set when the board may contain a run; cleared once it has been scanned. While
# it is clear the idle tick costs nothing, which is most of the time.
slot dirty 1

# Per-entity state the behaviour program keeps for itself. The engine has no
# idea these exist; they are just more slots after the entity pool.
#   velocityN     how many Q16.16 pixels entity N is falling this tick
array velocity 104 0

# The cells that are glimmering, and how many of the four slots are in use. A
# fixed pool, because the tick scans it rather than the board: the cost of a
# glimmer is then bounded no matter how many gems are on screen. Four is enough
# for runs to overlap without the board looking like a fireworks display.
slot glimmer-count 0
array glimmer-cell 4 -1

# Ticks since the program started. Animations are the engine's business, but
# starting one means writing the tick it began, so the world needs a clock.
slot time 0
# The tick at which the next glimmer is rolled for.
slot next-roll 0
